sub run_app($$) {
    my($app, $env) = @_;

    my $res;
    my $errors = '';

    eval {
        $res = $app->($env);
        1;
    } or do {
        $errors = $@;
    };

    if ($errors) {
        if (ref $env->{'psgi.errors'} eq 'GLOB') {
            print {$env->{'psgi.errors'}} $errors;
        }
        else {
            print $env->{'psgi.errors'}, $errors;
        }

        $res = [
            500,
            [ 'Content-Type' => 'text/plain' ],
            [ 'Internal Server Error' ]
        ];
    }

    return $res;
}
```

This function takes two parameters: `$app` and `$env`. It uses the Perl `eval` function to run the application and catch any errors. If an error occurs, it logs the error message to `$env->{'psgi.errors'}` (which is a file handle or a file name) and returns a 500 error response. If no error occurs, it returns the result of the application.

Please note that the `print` function is used to print the error message to the file handle or file name. If the `$env->{'psgi.errors'}` is a file handle, it is used as a file handle; if it is a file name, it is used as a file name. This is due to the fact that Perl does not differentiate between strings and file handles when printing.

